    sources: [{ id: "src_sheppard_wikidata", kind: "wikidata", title: "Kate Sheppard (Q455072), verified live via the Wikidata API (2026-11-20): English label 'Kate Sheppard', description 'prominent member of New Zealand's women's suffrage (1848-1934)' (the description's 1848 differs from the structured birth date), instance of human, born 10 March 1847, died 13 July 1934, citizenships United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland and New Zealand, Korean label 케이트 셰퍼드. Orientation only; not used as support for any row.", url: "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q455072" }, { id: "src_sheppard_smith1905", kind: "biography", title: "W. Sidney Smith, 'Outlines of the Women's Franchise Movement in New Zealand' (Christchurch: Whitcombe and Tombs, 1905; Internet Archive scan, OCR text read in full, with the plate captioned 'Mrs. K. W. Sheppard'). CANONICAL CONTEMPORARY ACCOUNT: the campaign told year by year by an insider-adjacent author (who married her in 1925), with a prefatory letter from Sir John Hall crediting Sheppard and her colleagues for 'judgment and tenacity'. Records her 1887 appointment as the Union's franchise superintendent at Mrs Leavitt's suggestion, the 1888 petition drawn up to catch the Electoral Bill, the 1889 comparison with Hall of members' positions and her consultation of the Unions by telegram on a property-qualification compromise, the local franchise superintendents, the 1891 petition of 10,085 signatures analysed by district, her 1893 telegram to the Legislative Council and letter to Governor Glasgow, and Seddon's telegram of 19 September 1893. Partisan and admiring; not independent of the movement.", url: "https://archive.org/details/outlinesofwomens00smit" }, { id: "src_sheppard_ccl", kind: "institution", title: "Christchurch City Libraries, 'Kate Sheppard (1847-1934)' local-history biography (read in full). Public-library biography that follows the Dictionary of New Zealand Biography: birth in Liverpool 'probably' 10 March 1847, arrival at Lyttelton in 1869, founding membership of the WCTU in 1885 and appointment as national franchise superintendent in 1887, petitions and the pamphlet Ten Reasons, failed bills in 1888, 1891 and 1892, a last petition of nearly 32,000 signatures, the Electoral Act of 19 September 1893, the 1895 White Ribbon newspaper, the 1896 National Council of Women, the resignation from active campaigning after 1902 for health reasons, and the summary that without her 'drive and energy' the vote 'would probably not have' come as soon. Admiring local-history source; derivative of the national biography.", url: "https://my.christchurchcitylibraries.com/kate-sheppard/" }, { id: "src_sheppard_britannica", kind: "biography", title: "Encyclopaedia Britannica, 'Kate Sheppard' (read in full): leader of the WCTU's suffrage campaign from 1887, wrote pamphlets, organised meetings and presented petitions, early causes of dress reform and cycling, first president of the National Council of Women in 1896 and stepped down in 1903 for poor health, later active in suffrage movements in England and the United States. Short reference article; consistent with the other biographies but adds no detail of its own.", url: "https://www.britannica.com/biography/Kate-Sheppard" }, { id: "src_sheppard_ebsco", kind: "biography", title: "Barb Lightner, 'Kate Sheppard', EBSCO Research Starters (2024; read in full). Reference summary built on the Dictionary of New Zealand Biography and NZHistory: her work through letters to newspapers, correspondence with suffragists abroad and a 'strategic alliance' with Sir John Hall; the 1891 women's page in The Prohibitionist written as 'Penelope' for three years; petitions of about 9,000 (1891), 19,000 (1892) and over 30,000 (1893) signatures; editorship of the White Ribbon from 1895 to 1903; first presidency of the National Council of Women in 1896; resignation in 1903 through ill health; the refusal for health reasons of an offered post as franchise superintendent of the World's WCTU. Derivative of the national biography; says a 'team of six hundred volunteers' gathered the 1893 signatures, a figure the library biography gives instead as the Union's membership, so the number is not used.", url: "https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/history/kate-sheppard/" }, { id: "src_sheppard_archifacts", kind: "institution", title: "Katrina Tamaira, 'Suffrage and Silences: Wahine Maori and the Vote', Archifacts (Archives and Records Association of New Zealand, 2022; read in full). Archival-studies essay that puts the petitions in context: at least seven suffrage petitions between 1888 and 1893 (778 signatures in 1888, about 9,000 in 1891, almost 20,000 in 1892, almost 32,000 in 1893, thirteen documents of which twelve small ones are lost), the petitions 'mainly organised by European women', the WCTU, Salvation Army and Dunedin Tailoresses' Union, and the very few identifiable Maori signatories (about ten) beside Meri Te Tai Mangakahia's parallel 1893 motion at the Kotahitanga parliament. Independent scholarly counter-perspective; not a biography of Sheppard.", url: "https://ojs.victoria.ac.nz/arch/article/download/10590/9297/17804" }, { id: "src_sheppard_rnz", kind: "press", title: "Sonia Sly, 'Women Caught Between Two Worlds', RNZ Beyond Kate (19 October 2018; read in full). Radio-programme feature in which Lizzie Marvelly and Libby Hakaraia discuss Maori suffragists and say that Maori women joining the WCTU-linked movement were presented with a pledge that, besides alcohol and tobacco, required giving up the moko kauae. COUNTER-VOICE on the movement Sheppard led, speaking of the movement and its pledge rather than of her own decisions; interview-based and opinion-led.", url: "https://www.rnz.co.nz/programmes/beyond-kate/story/2018667532/women-caught-between-two-worlds" }, { id: "src_sheppard_cclncw", kind: "institution", title: "Christchurch City Libraries blog, 'First National Council of Women, Christchurch, 1896' (13 April 2016; read in full). Describes the first National Council of Women, which met in Christchurch from 13 to 18 April 1896 as 'a world first - a national meeting of women who could vote in parliamentary elections', with Sheppard voted its first president and resolutions including equal divorce conditions, women jurors, old-age pensions and minimum wages. Library blog post; short.", url: "https://my.christchurchcitylibraries.com/blogs/post/first-national-council-of-women-christchurch-1896/" }],
